  "full_response": "Consensus algorithms are crucial components of distributed systems that ensure all the nodes in the network agree on a single source of truth, even in the presence of failures or network partitions. Raft is a consensus algorithm designed to be to understand while still being extremely robust and efficient. Here's how Raft works:\n\n1. Leader election: Raft divides time into elected terms, where each term is a fixed length in case a term's time elapses (i.e., considering node failures). The first (term 0) election is just a basic round-robin election to ensure fairness among the nodes to become a leader. As chaos ensues, the peers continuously (re-)election new leader. Terminal terms are not queued and ignored automatically.\n\n2. Leader election: Once a leader is elected in a term, it takes snapshots of the current stateavoiding shortcuts to voting). The newly elected leader then replicates the entire ordered log, bootstrapping future peers that are not yet initialized. Assuming the elected node is running properly, it not only becomes a leader but also an authoritative source (the leader) to store any required Log Entries.\n\n3. Log replication: The leader process propogates log entries (of the order Occurring before the split) n, dividing them evenly in all. Critically here, the nodes that the leader sends to act as replicators are the most recently-replicated log entries (peer logs that come just before or recursively for prior ones). If a node stops sending entries, it must be momentarily demoted (e.g., if other peers see bootstrapped log, the leader becomes a follower):\n\na) Read/Rollbacks: If a healed leader misses replication steps, it commits a Read/Rollback without log-conflict, adding the only missing subset number.\n\nb) Node's Action: Upon completion of replication, Nodes confirm primary or handle minorities essentially.\n\n4. Safety and correctness: Raft guarantees Partition Tolerance (according to the CAP theorem.) Log entries by a leader are acknowledged to be binary-safe, i.e., just self/set entries (e.g. leader: 1, follower: 0, committed status = 1).\n\nIn summary, Raft ensures that nodes reach an agreement on log entries, even in case of failures like loss or mistakes with eventual consistency and robustness going hand-in-hand. Typically used in distributed systems like Datastores like etcd andKeeper, ensuring rapid and reliable consensus in P2P networks, distributed coordination groups, or any decentralized",
